{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abe380a3963e86c7","type":"tool_result","content":"16:Red Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI) 3.6-ea.1 is a comprehensive AI/ML platform deployed on OpenShift Container Platform through the Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM). The platform is orchestrated by rhods-operator, which manages the lifecycle of all platform components through a hierarchy of custom resources — DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs. Each major capability (model serving, pipelines, distributed workloads, notebooks, model registry, training, observability, guardrails, and models-as-a-service) is implemented by a dedicated module operator or controller that watches its component CR and reconciles downstream Kubernetes resources using kustomize-based manifest rendering through the shared odh-platform-utilities framework.\n18:The platform ingress architecture is in active transition. The rhods-operator Gateway service controller provisions a central `data-science-gateway` using Gateway API (GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoutes) while maintaining backward compatibility through OpenShift Routes. kube-auth-proxy provides OIDC-authenticated access to the dashboard, and the Gateway controller conditionally integrates with Istio by dynamically creating EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when Istio CRDs are detected. Models-as-a-Service introduces a parallel ingress path through Kuadrant AuthPolicy enforcement with API key, TokenReview, and optional OIDC JWT authentication at the Gateway level. Model-registry-operator implements dual ingress (Gateway API HTTPRoutes and OpenShift Routes) with kube-rbac-proxy sidecar authentication on backend pods.\n20:Security follows a defense-in-depth posture: all shipped Go binaries are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptography through system OpenSSL, operators read the cluster APIServer TLS security profile to align their TLS configuration with platform-wide policy, admission webhooks validate and default CRD resources with fail-closed policies, and per-component RBAC is scoped through dedicated ServiceAccounts and ClusterRoles. Authentication is layered across gateway-level (kube-auth-proxy OIDC, Kuadrant AuthPolicy), pod-level (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar), and application-level (Kubernetes TokenReview, SubjectAccessReview) enforcement points.\n27:rhods-operator\n45:  mcp-lifecycle-module-operator\n46:  mcp-lifecycle-operator\n51:  ogx-k8s-operator\n54:  spark-operator\n56:  trainer-operator\n58:  training-operator\n65:**rhods-operator** is the platform root: it owns all component CRDs (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) and three service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that manage cross-cutting platform infrastructure. Every component operator depends on rhods-operator for activation and lifecycle management.\n67:**kserve** is the most connected serving component with 13 CRDs, 4 independent controller binaries (manager, llmisvc, localmodel, kserve-module), and integration with Gateway API, KEDA, cert-manager, Istio, and the Gateway API Inference Extension. Both rhods-operator and model-registry depend on it.\n79:| CRD-based orchestration | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, models-as-a-service, kuberay, kueue-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, spark-operator, model-registry-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, ai-gateway-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, trustyai-service-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator | Each component defines cluster-scoped CRDs watched by the platform operator; component operators reconcile their own CRDs into namespace-scoped workloads |\n80:| ODH module operator pattern | kserve (kserve-module), ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, ogx-k8s-operator | Module operators embed odh-platform-utilities framework for standardized manifest rendering, deploy actions, garbage collection, and status reporting |\n81:| Gateway API routing | rhods-operator, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, kserve (llmisvc), odh-model-controller | HTTPRoute resources reference the data-science-gateway for authenticated external access; components own their routes and bind to a shared gateway |\n82:| kube-rbac-proxy sidecar | kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator (monitoring proxies), odh-dashboard | TLS-terminating authentication proxy injected as a sidecar; validates tokens via Kubernetes TokenReview/SubjectAccessReview |\n83:| Conditional CRD watch | kserve, models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator | Controllers check for CRD availability before registering watches, enabling graceful degradation when optional platform features are not installed |\n84:| Platform TLS profile alignment | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, model-registry-operator, workbenches-operator, trainer | Operators read the OpenShift APIServer TLS security profile and apply matching cipher suites and minimum TLS version to their own endpoints |\n85:| Kuadrant policy integration | models-as-a-service, odh-model-controller, ai-gateway-operator | Kuadrant AuthPolicy and TokenRateLimitPolicy resources provide gateway-level authentication, authorization, and rate limiting |\n93:| redhat-ods-operator / opendatahub-operator-system | Operator namespace | rhods-operator |\n94:| redhat-ods-applications / opendatahub | Platform applications | odh-dashboard, odh-model-controller, kserve controllers, models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, notebooks, data-science-pipelines-operator, training-operator, trustyai-service-operator, kueue-operator, ai-gateway-operator |\n98:| models-as-a-service | MaaS namespace | maas-api, maas-controller; rhods-operator Auth controller watches this namespace |\n99:| kuadrant-system | Kuadrant infrastructure | Authorino/Kuadrant controllers; rhods-operator Auth controller watches this namespace |\n105:| data-science-gateway (openshift-ingress) | rhods-operator Gateway service controller | HTTPRoutes from models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, rhods-operator, kserve llmisvc | Envoy (via Istio/SAIL or gateway-api controller) | Conditional: EnvoyFilter -> ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy for dashboard; Kuadrant AuthPolicy for MaaS; kube-rbac-proxy sidecar for model-registry | odh-dashboard, models-as-a-service, model-registry, kserve LLMInferenceService | derived |\n106:| OpenShift Routes (various namespaces) | rhods-operator, model-registry-operator, odh-observability, trustyai-service-operator | Route resources per component | OpenShift HAProxy router | TLS termination at router; kube-rbac-proxy at pod level | odh-dashboard, model-registry REST, Prometheus proxies, TrustyAI service | observed |\n113:| data-science-gateway | openshift-ingress / platform GatewayClass | rhods-operator Gateway service controller | odh-dashboard (via kube-auth-proxy), models-as-a-service, model-registry-operator, kserve llmisvc, odh-model-controller | Per-route: EnvoyFilter ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy (dashboard), Kuadrant AuthPolicy (MaaS), kube-rbac-proxy sidecar (model-registry) | central/shared | derived |\n118:RHOAI 3.6-ea.1 presents a multi-generation ingress architecture. The primary ingress path uses Gateway API through the `data-science-gateway`, provisioned by the rhods-operator Gateway service controller. This gateway serves as the central platform ingress node, with multiple independently owned components attaching HTTPRoutes to it. The rhods-operator creates the GatewayClass and Gateway resources, and conditionally integrates with Istio by dynamically owning EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when Istio CRDs are detected on the cluster.\n120:A parallel ingress path through OpenShift Routes persists for backward compatibility and for components that have not yet migrated to Gateway API. The model-registry-operator explicitly implements dual ingress, creating both HTTPRoutes (referencing data-science-gateway) and OpenShift Routes, with the backend port selection conditionally routing through kube-rbac-proxy when configured.\n124:The EnvoyFilter -> ext_authz -> kube-auth-proxy chain for dashboard access is conditionally created by the rhods-operator Gateway controller when Istio CRDs are present. The specific filter configuration (selector, target service/port, auth-check path, route scope) is dynamically generated by the controller. Source inspection confirms that the Gateway controller owns EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources and deploys a kube-auth-proxy with OIDC support, but the exact ext_authz filter wiring details are rendered at reconciliation time from Go code rather than static manifests, making the full chain `derived` rather than `observed`. [source: rhods-operator internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:42-103]\n134:| OpenShift Routes (model-registry) | Bearer token | None (OpenShift router handles TLS termination only) | None | None at route level | kube-rbac-proxy sidecar: SubjectAccessReview for Service get permission in target namespace | kube-rbac-proxy deployment is conditional on operator configuration |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93b89d282589a824","agent_id":"abb90b1e07d9c0812","session_id":"4f0c1725-de57-4c53-a64c-2ffcce58d254","uuid":"ad191d1c-8c8a-491c-bff9-0f10ec0dbb73","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:32:06.192Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-abe380a3963e86c7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
