ments (DSC/component-controller resourceKind for queryability; document and publish the convention).\n- **Dependencies**: controller-runtime logr interface (internal, exists); COO team adoption (external, unknown, voluntary — not a gating dependency for any RHOAI deliverable).\n- **Acceptance Criteria**: Given/When/Then with measured-by clauses (CI lint rule/static analysis, unit tests on test-logger output, grep for removed key names).\n- **NFRs**: Backwards compatibility (additive fields, before/after log comparison, log-consumer ingestion verification, SRE/Support coordination on text-based alert rules); Performance (no verification required).\n\nAll required sections are present in the strategy. No health warning for missing sections.\n\n## Step 1.5 — Staff Engineer / SME Input Overrides\n\nThe strategy contains a non-empty Staff Engineer / SME Input section; it takes precedence and the following overrides are incorporated:\n\n| # | SME correction | Effect on decomposition |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------|\n| 1 | \"~16 component action files\" is wrong: exactly 8 component action files; 15 total action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | Manager-binary epic scope uses the confirmed 15-file / 4-tier breakdown |\n| 2 | Module controller tier (8 module handlers) was missing from the architecture description and HLRs | Module controller + 8 handlers + 3 module action files included in manager-binary epic scope (HLR coverage) |\n| 3 | Open Question 5 (action-file count) was based on a wrong premise — all 8 components have exactly one action file (8/8 confirmed) | Not treated as a genuine unknown; no Investigation epic created for it |\n| 4 | Confirmed three-problem diagnosis, DSCI key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), cloudmanager shared reconciler already emitting name/namespace, camelCase convention | Decomposition follows the corrected architecture; cloudmanager epic scoped to resourceKind + embedded-identity conversion |\n\n## Step 2 — Component Graph\n\n- **rhods-operator** (canonical component mapping below): Active — code changes in both binaries → generates Implementation epics (E001 manager binary, E002 cloudmanager binary).\n- **controller-runtime logr**: existing internal foundation, not a component — no epic.\n- **COO operators (Loki, Tempo)**: external, voluntary adoption per platform policy overlay 0008 — no epic, no dependency edge; covered by the publication deliverable in E003.\n- No new components: rhods-operator is in architecture context (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`), so no onboarding chain is generated.\n\n**Canonical component mapping (non-obvious):** the strategy names `rhods-operator`, which is not a verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. The closest canonical component is **`AI Core Platform`** — rhods-operator is the RHOAI platform root operator (per PLATFORM.md: \"rhods-operator is the platform root\"), the central control plane of the AI core platform. All three epics use `AI Core Platform`. Alternative considered: `OpenShift AI Productization` (the repo is the downstream productization of opendatahub-operator), rejected because the epic work targets the platform's core operator code and its logging surfaces, not the productization/sync process.\n\n## Step 3 — Investigation Epic Analysis\n\nAll unknowns were scanned from the Open Questions table, Risks, Assumptions, and Prerequisites. None qualify as Investigation epics:\n\n| Unknown | Owner | Changes which epics exist / what they do? | Handling |\n|---------|-------|-------------------------------------------|----------|\n| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind? | PM / Observability | No — HLRs fix the required field set; extra fields would be an optional extension documented in the guideline | Open question on E003 (guideline content decision); core conversion unaffected |\n| Which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns? | SRE / Support | No — identification happens during regression validation before merge | Acceptance criterion + risk note on E001/E002 (coordinate with SRE/Support) |\n| Cloudmanager in same release or subsequent? | PM / ODH Operator | No — the cloudmanager epic exists either way; this is release scheduling | Health Warning; E002 is independently schedulable precisely because it is a separate epic |\n| Actual percentage of embedded log calls (75% claimed)? | ODH Operator | No — the audit identifies call sites; conversion proceeds identically at any percentage | Audit is the first scope item of E001/E002; drives the re-split trigger in Health Warnings |\n| Prerequisites pending human review (architecture review, etc.) | Process gates | No — approval gates, not design forks | Health Warning (process gates pending) |\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Structured resource context in rhods-operator manager binary reconciliation error logs | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Structured resource context in rhods-operator cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Structured log field convention contribution guideline and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 manager binary log standardization]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 cloudmanager binary log standardization]\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 convention guideline and publication]\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|-----------------|------|-----------|\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas: manager binary (5 controller tiers, 15 action files) and cloudmanager binary (3 infrastructure controllers on the shared reconciler) touch disjoint code paths. Merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. Both deliver independent partial value. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic blocked by ALL Implementation epics: the guideline documents the convention as implemented in the manager binary (including the DSCI key migration guide and before/after examples drawn from the actual conversions). |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic blocked by ALL Implementation epics: the published convention must reflect what both binaries implement. |\n| CI lint rule: no standalone epic | Step 5 shared-surface rule | The strategy's CI lint rule (flag `log.Error` calls missing required structured fields) is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable. It is divided per surface, not per work type: E001 delivers and verifies lint coverage for the manager binary's code paths; E002 delivers and verifies lint coverage for the cloudmanager binary's code paths. No standalone lint epic — that would carve one logical change into a work-type epic and leave each implementation epic's slice incomplete. |\n| No Investigation epics | Step 3 | No unknown changes epic existence or content (see table above). The call-site audit is the first scope item of each implementation epic. |\n| No COO-team epic or edge | Rule 21 / platform policy | External adoption is voluntary per overlay 0008; RHOAI delivers full value without it. Publication (tracking issues in COO projects) is a deliverable of E003, not a dependency. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | E001 (manager), E002 (cloudmanager) | The HLR spans both binaries; each epic converts the call sites in its own binary. |\n| DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller is manager-binary scope. |\n| Service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | Service controllers are manager-binary scope. |\n| All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module action files) | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files live in the manager binary's controller tree. |\n| Cloudmanager controller (AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | Cloudmanager-binary scope; shared reconciler already provides name/namespace. |\n| Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 | Module controller tier is manager-binary scope. |\n| DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (queryability enhancement) | P1 | E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 conversion in E001: the resourceKind field is added to the very same `log.Error` call sites being converted for the P0 embedded-identity HLRs — same controllers, same call sites, same unit-test surface. Splitting it out would force a second pass over every controller file and create an artificial serialization edge between two edits of the same lines. Not independently deferrable: shipping the conversion without resourceKind on DSC/component controllers would leave the cross-controller query capability (the strategy's Problem 3) half-delivered and require re-touching all converted call sites. |\n| Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 | Substantive new documentation content — distinct work stream, separate docs-authoring epic. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Epic-size overage on E001 (managed, shape 2 — one logical change over an enumerated surface).** The manager-binary epic carries the audit (~0.5 sprint) plus conversion across 5 controller tiers and 15 action files (~0.75–1 sprint) — at or slightly above the ~2-week epic guideline. It is kept intact because it is a single mechanical sweep (one structured-log conversion applied uniformly to every call site in an enumerated file set, presented by the strategy as one work item); splitting by controller tier would fragment one logical change and force multiple passes over shared test/lint surfaces. **Conditional re-split trigger:** if the validated audit of affected `log.Error` call sites materially exceeds the strategy's 75% estimate (i.e., the conversion load meaningfully exceeds the ~1.5-sprint estimate), or capacity constraints require independently schedulable units, re-split E001 along controller-tier seams (e.g., component+DSC controllers and their action files vs. service+module+DSCI controllers and their action files), each sub-epic carrying its own lint-coverage slice.\n2. **P1 bundled in P0 epic.** The DSC/component-controller `resourceKind` HLR (P1) is bundled in E001 — rationale recorded in the HLR Traceability Matrix row (same call sites, same test surface; splitting costs a second pass over every converted file).\n3. **Release-scheduling open question.** Whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as manager or a subsequent one (PM decision) does not change epic structure — E002 is independently schedulable because it is a separate epic with no DAG edge to E001. E003 must follow whichever implementations ship in the release it documents.\n4. **Process gates pending.** All Prerequisites (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, License Validation, etc.) are \"Pending human review\" in the strategy — standard approval gates, not design forks; no decomposition impact, but they gate execution start.\n5. **Scope trap checked — none found.** The backwards-compatibility NFR's dev-cluster validation (test build with production log-consumer stack, SRE/Support coordination on text-based alert rules) is within the ODH Operator team's engineering control and is carried as an acceptance criterion on E001/E002; it does not imply infrastructure outside the platform inventory.\n6. **COO adoption timing is external and voluntary** (per overlay 0008) — tracked as a risk on E003 (publish early, file tracking issues), not as a dependency or AC.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing gates RHOAI deliverable value: the log standardization delivers its full user value (queryable structured fields in rhods-operator logs) without COO adoption, which is voluntary per platform policy. E001 and E002 run in parallel (independent partial value); E003 follows both.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51699,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93b89d282589a824","agent_id":"abb90b1e07d9c0812","session_id":"4f0c1725-de57-4c53-a64c-2ffcce58d254","uuid":"e6013920-1adf-4ea8-801d-54a020d5880c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:34:29.714Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
