{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9901639b-b35f-418e-bda8-025106bfe1dc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b67322f65dea7e2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-19nkc8wv/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E001.md","content":"# Structured resource context in rhods-operator manager binary reconciliation error logs\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the structured resource-context log convention across the rhods-operator **manager binary**: every reconciliation error log in the manager binary carries resource identity as structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) instead of embedding it in human-readable message strings. It resolves the three problems diagnosed in the strategy within the manager binary: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, and the missing `resourceKind` field.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Audit** all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager binary's five controller tiers, using grep/static analysis to produce a coverage checklist of call sites embedding resource identity in message strings.\n- **Convert message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs** (`name`, `namespace`) at every audited call site in the manager binary, removing the redundant identity from the message text where it has been moved to structured fields.\n- **Add `resourceKind`** as a structured field to reconciliation error logs of all manager-binary controllers:\n  - DSC controller (DataScienceCluster) and 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) — for cross-controller queryability;\n  - 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup);\n  - module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator);\n  - DSCI controller.\n- **Migrate DSCI key names** from non-standard `Request.Name` / `DSCInitialization` to the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`), per the confirmed three-problem diagnosis.\n- **Convert action-file error logs** across all 4 tiers — 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`), 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`), 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`), 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) — to include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields. Total: 15 action files.\n- **Cluster-scoped resource handling**: for cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization), emit `name` and `resourceKind` and omit `namespace` (or set it to empty string) rather than emitting a spurious value.\n- **CI lint rule for the manager binary's code paths**: add and verify a lint rule / static-analysis check that flags any `log.Error` call in manager-binary code missing the required structured fields or embedding name/namespace in message text, enforced in CI for this epic's surfaces.\n- **Backwards-compatibility validation**: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error; deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log-consumer stack configured, trigger reconciliation errors, and verify the log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes; coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update any text-based alert rules that rely on changed message-string patterns.\n\nField naming follows camelCase (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) per the Staff Engineer / SME input, aligning with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions. Framework controllers already emit `name`/`namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger at the reconcile entry point; this epic adds `resourceKind` and converts the error-path call sites.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a reconciliation error log call in any manager-binary controller that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the log call uses structured key-value pairs instead — measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis flagging zero remaining `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text in manager-binary code paths.\n- Given the DSCI controller's reconciliation error logs, when the code is updated, then all structured field names use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) — measured by a unit test asserting DSCI error logs contain the standard field names and a codebase grep confirming no references to `Request.Name` / `DSCInitialization` as log field names remain.\n- Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `resourceKind` as a structured field — measured by a unit test parsing test logger output and asserting `resourceKind` is present with the correct value.\n- Given an action file error log across any of the 4 tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module action files), when the code is updated, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields — measured by unit tests asserting all three fields are present with correct values across all 15 action files.\n- Given the module controller reconciles any of its 8 module handlers and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` — measured by unit tests on test logger output.\n- Given the DSC controller or a component controller encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `resourceKind` — measured by unit tests on test logger output.\n- Given a cluster-scoped resource (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` and `namespace` is omitted or empty — measured by a unit test confirming cluster-scoped resources do not emit a spurious namespace value.\n- Given a sample reconciliation error before and after this epic's changes, when the log outputs are compared and ingested by the production log-consumer stack on a dev cluster, then structured fields appear and the log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes — measured by a documented before/after comparison from the dev-cluster test build, including identification (with SRE/Support) of any text-based alert rules requiring updates.\n- The CI lint rule fails the build for any new `log.Error` call in manager-binary code paths missing the required structured fields — measured by the rule running in CI with a demonstrated failure case.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- \\[P0\\] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — manager-binary call sites.\n- \\[P0\\] DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`).\n- \\[P0\\] All service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` as a structured field.\n- \\[P0\\] All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields across all 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module action files).\n- \\[P0\\] Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields (8 module handlers).\n- \\[P1\\] DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability (bundled: