{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d3623673-0431-4a70-835a-f1c29dc6b369","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1b0f040094883a0","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-19nkc8wv/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# Structured log field convention contribution guideline and external publication\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the documentation of the structured resource-context log convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator, and publishes the convention for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator). Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies — adoption is at the external teams' discretion, so publication (not adoption) is the deliverable.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline (inside the guideline / design document):\n\n- **Required structured fields** for reconciliation error logs: `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`, with cluster-scoped-resource handling (omit or empty `namespace`).\n- **camelCase naming rationale** — alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions.\n- **The three-problem diagnosis**: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field.\n- **Before/after code examples** for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs, drawn from the completed conversions in the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n- **DSCI key migration guide**: release-notes documentation of the `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` → `name`/`namespace` migration with before/after query examples, noting that the transition happens in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency.\n- **Contribution guideline** framing for new controller development: what a new controller's error logs must include and how the CI lint rule enforces it.\n- **Open content question to resolve during authoring** (PM / Observability team decision): whether the convention should recommend optional fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category), weighing utility against log volume.\n\nPublication activities:\n\n- Publish the convention as a specification for external operator adoption and file tracking issues in the COO projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) so adoption can be tracked; accept that external adoption is best-effort per platform policy.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The contribution guideline exists and specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind) — measured by review of the published guideline against this content list.\n- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and a DSCI key-name migration guide with before/after query examples — measured by review of the guideline's examples against the completed manager-binary conversions.\n- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented log changes in both the manager and cloudmanager binaries (examples and field names match what the code actually emits) — measured by a documented technical review sign-off.\n- The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the Loki Operator and Tempo Operator projects — measured by links to the published specification and the filed tracking issues.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- \\[P1\\] Document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"i