{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae8ae85acead9f34","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Structured log field convention contribution guideline and external publication\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: AI Core Platform\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\tai_signals:\n14\t  change_specificity: 1\n15\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n16\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n17\t  existing_foundation: 0\n18\t  open_questions: -1\n19\t  external_dependency: 0\n20\t  human_process_gates: -1\n21\t  repo_access: 1\n22\t  architecture_claims: 1\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: Medium\n25\tai_implementability_score: 1\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Structured log field convention contribution guideline and external publication\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tThis epic delivers the documentation of the structured resource-context log convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator, and publishes the convention for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator). Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies — adoption is at the external teams' discretion, so publication (not adoption) is the deliverable.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\tContent outline (inside the guideline / design document):\n40\t\n41\t- **Required structured fields** for reconciliation error logs: `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`, with cluster-scoped-resource handling (omit or empty `namespace`).\n42\t- **camelCase naming rationale** — alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions.\n43\t- **The three-problem diagnosis**: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field.\n44\t- **Before/after code examples** for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs, drawn from the completed conversions in the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n45\t- **DSCI key migration guide**: release-notes documentation of the `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` → `name`/`namespace` migration with before/after query examples, noting that the transition happens in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency.\n46\t- **Contribution guideline** framing for new controller development: what a new controller's error logs must include and how the CI lint rule enforces it.\n47\t- **Open content question to resolve during authoring** (PM / Observability team decision): whether the convention should recommend optional fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category), weighing utility against log volume.\n48\t\n49\tPublication activities:\n50\t\n51\t- Publish the convention as a specification for external operator adoption and file tracking issues in the COO projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) so adoption can be tracked; accept that external adoption is best-effort per platform policy.\n52\t\n53\t## Acceptance Criteria\n54\t\n55\t- The contribution guideline exists and specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind) — measured by review of the published guideline against this content list.\n56\t- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and a DSCI key-name migration guide with before/after query examples — measured by review of the guideline's examples against the completed manager-binary conversions.\n57\t- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented log changes in both the manager and cloudmanager binaries (examples and field names match what the code actually emits) — measured by a documented technical review sign-off.\n58\t- The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the Loki Operator and Tempo Operator projects — measured by links to the published specification and the filed tracking issues.\n59\t\n60\t## HLR Traceability\n61\t\n62\t- \\[P1\\] Document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n63\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d50220eae4577bc","agent_id":"a8b8f8b1baed9e039","session_id":"4f0c1725-de57-4c53-a64c-2ffcce58d254","uuid":"0577735f-2a8e-436a-8c65-759f6dac38bf","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:37:35.693Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae8ae85acead9f34","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
