{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c9719d40ab020da","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: rhods-operator serves as the central control plane for Red Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI). It defines and reconciles a hierarchy of custom resources—DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs such as DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, and KServe—that declaratively describe the desired state of the AI/ML platform. The operator deploys and manages each component's manifests via kustomize-based rendering and template actions, while a set of service controllers handle cross-cutting concerns: the Auth service controller manages RBAC group policies and watches for dependent namespaces (models-as-a-service, kuadrant-system), the Gateway service controller provisions a data-science gateway with kube-auth-proxy OIDC infrastructure and conditionally integrates with Istio (EnvoyFilter, DestinationRule) and OpenShift OAuth, and the Monitoring service controller deploys Prometheus proxies and collectors for platform-level observability. A companion cloudmanager binary handles cloud-provider Kubernetes engine resources (AWS, Azure, CoreWeave). The operator exposes 19 CRDs, serves admission webhooks on port 9443 with service-ca TLS, and reads the cluster APIServer TLS profile to align its own TLS configuration with the platform-wide security policy. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, cmd/main.go:480-494, internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:56-88, internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:40-109, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]\n20:rhods-operator follows a layered controller-runtime reconciliation architecture with three distinct controller tiers: a top-level DataScienceCluster controller that owns the platform lifecycle, per-component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) that manage individual AI/ML workload components, and service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that handle cross-cutting platform infrastructure.\n48:| cloudmanager | Go executable | cmd/cloudmanager |\n49:| cmd | Go controller-runtime operator | cmd |\n82:| infrastructure.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | AWSKubernetesEngine | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n83:| infrastructure.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | AzureKubernetesEngine | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n84:| infrastructure.opendatahub.io | v1alpha1 | CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine | Cluster | Core API | Custom resource managed by rhods-operator |\n123:| github.com/go-logr/logr | v1.4.3 | Yes | runtime-observability | runtime-observability |\n139:| github.com/openshift/controller-runtime-common | v0.0.0-20260428152732-64ee174f5e2e | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n145:| github.com/sirupsen/logrus | v1.9.3 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n162:| sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime | v0.24.1 | Yes | runtime-framework | runtime-framework |\n168:| controller-runtime | 0.24.1 | Yes | Unknown | Operator framework |\n343:| Webhook (port 9443) | HTTPS | TLS serving certificate (server identity) | controller-runtime webhook server | API server validates the OpenShift service-ca serving certificate opendatahub-operator-controller-webhook-cert |\n361:| **Build flags (cloudmanager)** | CGO_ENABLED=${CGO_ENABLED} (variable), -tags strictfipsruntime | Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:59 |\n373:| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | cloudmanager CGO_ENABLED uses build arg variable rather than explicit `1`; if built without CGO the binary would use pure-Go crypto | Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:59 |\n375:The manager binary is built with explicit FIPS-compliant settings (`CGO_ENABLED=1`, `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime`), ensuring that Go's crypto operations delegate to OpenSSL on the host. The cloudmanager binary uses `-tags strictfipsruntime` but relies on a build argument for `CGO_ENABLED` rather than hardcoding it to `1`, which is a minor build-configuration risk if the argument is not provided. The operator reads the cluster-wide TLS security profile from the OpenShift APIServer and applies it to its own TLS configuration, ensuring alignment with platform TLS policy. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d50220eae4577bc","agent_id":"a8b8f8b1baed9e039","session_id":"4f0c1725-de57-4c53-a64c-2ffcce58d254","uuid":"f6215b18-6eb8-4c6e-96c3-5670ae58c3a6","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:37:53.773Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c9719d40ab020da","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
