{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c1636078c6e9c96","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tcontract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8010` exited 0 — epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all pass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) and epic_count is 3 — no triage contradiction; multi-epic output is expected. No conditional branch files exist, and no main-plan epic references a branch node.\n15\t\n16\t## Criterion Details\n17\t\n18\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n19\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: the 6 P0 HLRs map to E001 (manager-binary scope: embedded-identity conversion, DSCI key migration, service-controller resourceKind, 15 action files across 4 tiers, module controller + 8 handlers) and E002 (cloudmanager controllers), with the first P0 HLR correctly split across both binaries since it spans both. Both P1 HLRs are covered: the DSC/component-controller resourceKind HLR is bundled into E001 with a self-contained inline rationale in the HLR Traceability Matrix row (same call sites, same files, same unit-test surface — a shared implementation surface, so bundling is the compliant shape, not a deferrable-feature collapse), and the documentation HLR maps to E003. Priority inheritance is correct: E001/E002 are P0 and block no lower-priority implementation work; E003 is a docs-authoring epic (exempt from priority inheritance) whose P1 correctly derives from the strategy's Major Jira priority. Traceability matrix is present and complete.\n20\t\n21\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n22\tThe DAG is E001 → E003 and E002 → E003, exactly matching E003's frontmatter `dependencies` ([E001, E002]) and E001/E002's empty lists; diagram arrow direction (dependency → dependent) is correct. Both edges are Rule 11 edges (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) — valid, and not flagged as serialization. E001 ∥ E002 parallelism is justified (same repo, disjoint code paths: manager binary vs cloudmanager binary; independent partial value). No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 pairings are vacuously satisfied. No circular dependencies, no invalid edges, no missing data/artifact edges — E003's consumption of the completed conversions is captured by its existing edges to both implementation epics; the per-binary CI lint slices require no cross-epic edge since each epic enforces lint on its own surface. No `gated_by`/branch metadata to check. Critical path length 2 matches the S-size heuristic (1-2).\n23\t\n24\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n25\tAll three epics carry the correct component (AI Core Platform — a canonical entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, with the non-obvious mapping from `rhods-operator` documented and a rejected alternative recorded) and team (ODH Operator team). The manager/cloudmanager split is along a real architectural seam (two binaries, disjoint code paths) and directly serves the independent-schedulability requirement created by the strategy's release-scheduling open question — not a boundary violation. E001's size is at/slightly above the ~2-week guideline but is the compliant shape-2 sweep (one mechanical structured-log conversion applied uniformly to an enumerated file set, presented by the strategy as one work item) and Health Warning 1 carries a concrete conditional re-split trigger (audit exceeding the 75% estimate, with named tier seams) — per the review rules, no split is demanded and the criterion is not docked; recorded as a watch note only. No scope duplication: E001/E002 cover disjoint binaries, and the CI lint rule and backwards-compatibility validation are divided